# What the desktop user may do as root, which is three programs.
#
# The Web tab writes the chosen search engine into every browser on the
# machine, and all three of them read their policy out of /etc. The panel runs
# as them, so something has to carry it across, and this is the smallest thing
# that will: one program, which takes the name of an engine and writes three
# files. It has no way to be handed anything else to do. A name it does not
# know is a name it refuses rather than a name it passes on.
#
# Without a password because the alternative is a settings panel that stops to
# ask for one on a machine with no keyboard, and a password prompt nobody can
# answer is a setting that cannot be changed.
#
# `@user@` is the mark the manifest writes for whoever this desktop belongs to.
# `console apply` fills it in on the way here, because sudo wants a name.
@user@ ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/bin/console-engine
# The second is the setup screen's. Somebody moving the menu onto a button
# their device actually has writes that into a table in their own home, and the
# profiles it changes are in /etc, which is not theirs. `buttons` is the whole
# of what it may do: it takes no argument, what it writes is decided by that
# table and by the tree, and there is nothing to hand it that would make it
# write anything else. `console apply` is not this line and cannot be reached
# through it -- sudo matches the words as well as the program.
@user@ ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/bin/console buttons

# The third is the Language tab's and the Setup tab's. Which language this
# machine is in, where it keeps the hour and what it answers to are four files
# under /etc -- locale.gen, locale.conf, localtime and hostname -- and the
# panel is not root either. `console-machine` is the one door to them, and
# every word it takes is checked against a list the machine itself holds: a
# language has to be a line in /usr/share/i18n/SUPPORTED, an hour has to be a
# zone timedatectl names, and a name has to be one a network could look up. A
# word it does not know is refused rather than passed on.
#
# It could have gone through polkit instead -- timedatectl and hostnamectl both
# ask it -- and that is a password box on a machine whose only keyboard is
# drawn by the session that would be asking. The same argument as the first
# rule, for the same reason.
@user@ ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/bin/console-machine
